  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAs of 1:00 AM EST...Current sfc analysis shows areas of high \npressure centered to our south and to our north resulting in \ncontinued large scale upper level ridging. This will help allow for \nsome periods of clearing in the thick mid and high level clouds, \nshown in current GOES satellite imagery, as a weak upper level \ndisturbance passes though overnight this morning. Southerly flow \nwill also gradually increase available moisture. Because of the \nincreased cloud coverage, temperatures in more northern areas are \nrunning slightly warmer with this morning's lows in these areas in \nthe mid to upper 40s, while areas south and east where there are \nmore breaks in clouds will allow for lows to dip into the 30s.\n\nThis morning the surface high pressure slides off the Atlantic \ncoast while a low pressure and upper level trough over the Great \nPlains deepens and begins to slowly slide east. It's associated warm \nfront lifts though this afternoon. With the peak of the ridge \nalmost overhead, above normal temperatures are expected with high's \ntoday in the low upper 50s and low 60s (terrain) and upper 60s \njust reaching 70 (valleys). Although, moisture and Td continuing to \nincrease, it remains limited, and the best forcing looks to the N/NW \nof our region. A few light showers due to upsloping could result in \nthe southern ADKs this afternoon but look to be less than a \ntenth of an inch. For the rest of the region, a mainly dry and \npleasant day is expected. Tonight will also be somewhat mild \nwith lows in the upper 40s and low 50s. Some patchy fog \ncould develop in the sheltered valley areas tonight as \ndewpoints rise. Wednesday, the upper level ridge axis moves directly \noverhead and will be the warmest day this week. Model guidance \nshows 850 mb temperatures on Wednesday are about 13 to 15 degrees C \nand ensemble anomalies showing about +1 to +2 STDEV above normal. \nHighs are expected to reach the upper 60s to low 70s (normal high \ntemperature for this time of year is about 54 F). It will also be \nmore breezy (wind gusts up to 10-15 kt), especially in the N/S \noriented valleys. Dew points continue to creep up with southerly \nflow. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThursday, the low pressure system and cold front from the Great \nPlains tracks into the western Great Lakes region and occludes. \nPWATs rise to around 1 to 1.5\" slightly above the 75th \nclimatological percentile. Showers are expected to the arrive first \nin the southern ADKs and areas north and west of I-90 Thursday \nmorning ahead of the cold front before overspreading the region from \nwest to east. Western areas, especially the southern ADKs, still \nhave the best chance to see (40-60%) to see 0.50\" or greater of \nrain. Latest long range deterministic guidance continue to show the \ntrend of weakening showers into the afternoon as they track east \nover the Capital Region into Thursday evening, outrunning the better \nforcing along the front. Here, rainfall amounts Thursday generally \nlook to be under a quarter inch, and possibly closer to a tenth of \nan inch for the Mid Hudson Valley and southwestern New England. \nAdditional chances for light showers linger Thursday night into \nFriday as the front slows and stalls with a wave of low pressure \ndeveloping along the front near the New England coast. The latest \nensemble guidance has the best chance (60-70%) for 0.5\" or greater of \nrain through Friday night looks to be areas south and east of I-90, \ncloser to the New England coast. There is still some uncertainty as \nto the timing of the front and resulting rainfall, Given very\ndry weather over the last 2 weeks, any rain will be quite \nbeneficial with no concerns at all for hydro issues. Even if we \ndon't see the steadier/heavier rain with the coastal low system, \nscattered showers and cooler weather are still expected as the \nupper trough/upper low and cold front tracks overhead Friday into \nthe weekend.",
